Neurofibromatosis
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Six or more café-au-lait macules, Multiple cutaneous neurofibromas, Axillary/inguinal freckling

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-031
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of intense, unmanageable radicular pain originating from a known plexiform neurofibroma.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented severe, sudden-onset, and unremitting neuropathic pain radiating from a previously stable, deep paraspinal mass, causing functional immobility and extreme distress. Urgent in-person assessment is required to provide potent analgesia, exclude acute malignant transformation or acute spinal cord compression, and coordinate rapid advanced imaging before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The lump in my lower back is suddenly burning like fire, and the pain is shooting all the way down my right leg. I haven't slept and I can barely walk."
History of Present Illness: The patient has an established diagnosis of Neurofibromatosis Type 1, with a strong, clearly documented family history strongly indicating a prominent autosomal dominant pattern of inheritance. The patient has multiple distinctly hyperpigmented, flat cutaneous café-au-lait macules and multiple soft, fleshy, and distinctly palpable cutaneous neurofibromas widely distributed indiscriminately across the body surface. The current presentation involves a known plexiform neurofibroma located in the right lumbosacral paraspinal region. Over the past 48 hours, there has been an aggressive, rapid escalation of severe, sharp radicular pain radiating into the right lower extremity, suggesting the tumor mass is infiltrating or compressing deep along nerve trunks. The pain is severe, constant, and significantly limits mobility. The patient denies sudden lower limb weakness or bowel/bladder incontinence.
Functional Status: Acutely restricted mobility; utilizing a cane to offload the right leg due to severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls due to pain-related guarding; severe sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, shifting weight constantly, prominent cutaneous stigmata of NF1 visible.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals the diagnostic tetrad elements: more than six café-au-lait macules, multiple cutaneous neurofibromas, and prominent axillary freckling (Crowe sign) strictly located hidden in deep skin folds. Palpation of the right lumbosacral region reveals a firm, large, diffuse plexiform neurofibroma that is exquisitely tender to deep palpation. Neurological examination of the right lower limb demonstrates intact power (5/5) but marked hyperalgesia in the L5/S1 dermatomes. Straight leg raise is positive on the right at 40 degrees, exacerbating the radicular pain. Reflexes are symmetrical. No saddle anesthesia or reduced anal tone.
Environmental Observations: Heating pad continuously applied to the back; over-the-counter analgesia packets empty on the table.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and Ibuprofen providing zero relief. Gabapentin 300mg TDS is current but inadequate for this acute flare.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Neurofibromatosis Type 1 - Status: Acute Neuropathic Pain Flare (Suspected acute compression by plexiform neurofibroma)
Differentials:

Acute Malignant Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumor (MPNST) Transformation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While a critical consideration, the rapid onset over 48 hours is more indicative of acute mechanical compression/inflammation than sudden macroscopic malignant growth, though urgent MRI is required to definitively exclude this.

Acute Lumbar Disc Herniation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain precisely localizes to and radiates from the known, highly tender paraspinal plexiform mass rather than the central spine.

Herpes Zoster (Shingles) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a vesicular rash in the affected dermatome and the pain originating deep from a palpable mass.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, agonizing neuropathic pain crisis originating from a known plexiform neurofibroma. The severity of the pain and the potential for rapid neurological compromise (or malignant transformation) mandated immediate clinical attention. Deferring assessment would result in catastrophic pain levels, prolonged immobility, and delayed diagnosis of a potentially life-threatening complication. Urgent after-hours assessment safely ruled out immediate cauda equina syndrome or catastrophic motor deficit, allowing for the rapid escalation of targeted neuropathic analgesia on-site and the facilitation of an urgent outpatient MRI to evaluate the structural integrity of the nerve root, strictly meeting MBS urgency criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Tapentadol 50mg PRN for acute severe breakthrough pain. Increased Gabapentin to 600mg TDS for 7 days to aggressively target the neuropathic mechanism. Arranged an urgent, fast-tracked MRI of the lumbosacral spine and plexus.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By strictly avoiding all heavy lifting or bending for the next 72 hours, the patient prevents additional mechanical stress on the inflamed nerve trunk, immediately reducing the frequency of shooting pain spikes.
Step 2: By setting a strict phone alarm to take the Gabapentin precisely every 8 hours, the patient maintains a constant blood level of the drug, actively preventing the agonizing pain flares that occur when the medication wears off.
Step 3: By alternating a cold pack (for 15 minutes) with the heating pad, the patient actively reduces deep tissue inflammation around the tumor mass, directly decreasing the mechanical pressure on the trapped nerve.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Urgent review with the primary GP and treating Neurologist within 3 days post-MRI.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed immediately to the Emergency Department if you develop sudden numbness between your legs, lose control of your bladder or bowels, or if your leg suddenly becomes completely weak and unable to bear weight (foot drop).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute changes in plexiform neurofibromas must always be evaluated by MRI to rule out malignant transformation.
Clinical Course: The stronger medications will significantly blunt the shooting pain within 24 hours, allowing for mobility. The urgent MRI will dictate if surgical or oncological intervention is necessary.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for review if the mass suddenly visibly expands in size over hours, or if the skin over the mass becomes red, hot, and exquisitely tender.

